# Runbook VI — Anti-Slop & Backpressure for the Solo Agentic Practitioner

*A standalone, rules-first handbook for a senior AI engineering practitioner working solo in a single-operator-plus-agent loop — building software WITH coding agents and designing/building production agentic systems. Sixth in the runbook series. Overlap with prior runbooks is intentional; this stands alone.*

## TL;DR
- **Generation now vastly outpaces both verification and comprehension. The two disciplines that keep a solo human-plus-agent loop controllable are (1) reducing slop at the source (constrain what the agent produces) and (2) applying backpressure (pace and gate what the agent is allowed to do). Neither alone is sufficient.** The data is unambiguous: GitClear's 2026 data shows copy/paste rose to 15.7% of changed lines while refactoring collapsed to 3.8%; Faros AI (10,000+ devs) found AI-heavy teams merged 98% more PRs but review time rose 91% and DORA metrics did not move.
- **Slop is not a style problem; it is a maintainability and review-burden problem with measured cost.** Treat every abstraction, dependency, and line as guilty until proven earning-its-keep, and run automated "sensors" (lint, duplication, complexity, dead-code) plus a mandatory anti-slop cleanup pass before shipping.
- **Backpressure is real engineering discipline (Nygard's stability patterns, Kanban/Little's Law, TCP-style flow control) that maps cleanly onto BOTH your workflow (WIP limits, review as the bottleneck, circuit-breakers on thrashing agents) AND the systems you build (bounded queues, rate limits, token budgets, bulkheads, load shedding).** The human review step is the rate-limiting resource — design the whole loop around it.

## Key Findings

**The generation–verification asymmetry is now quantified and worsening.** This is the throughline of 2026 AI-engineering discourse. METR's July 2025 randomized controlled trial (arXiv 2507.09089; Becker, Rush, Barnes, Rein) studied 16 experienced open-source developers averaging ~5 years of experience on their own repos, across 246 tasks, using Cursor Pro with Claude 3.5/3.7 Sonnet. The finding: "developers forecast that allowing AI will reduce completion time by 24%… After completing the study, developers estimate that allowing AI reduced completion time by 20%… we find that allowing AI actually increases completion time by 19%." Perception and reality were inverted. Faros AI's 2025 telemetry (10,000+ developers, 1,255 teams) found high-AI-adoption teams completed 21% more tasks and merged 98% more PRs, but PR review time rose 91%, average PR size grew 154%, bug counts rose 9%, and organizational DORA metrics were flat. The March 2026 Faros follow-up ("The Acceleration Whiplash," 22,000 devs, 4,000 teams) showed the pattern compounding: "Bugs per developer are up 54%. The incidents-to-PR ratio has more than tripled. Median review time has increased 5X. 31% more PRs are merging without any review." This is Goldratt's Theory of Constraints and Amdahl's Law in practice: speeding up a non-bottleneck (generation) while the bottleneck (human review/comprehension) is unchanged yields no system throughput gain.

**Slop has a measured maintainability cost.** GitClear's longitudinal work (211M+ lines from Google/Microsoft/Meta and OSS repos): copy/paste rose from 8.3% (2020) to 12.3% (2024), then to 15.7% of changed lines (H1 2026); "moved"/refactored code (the signature of reuse) fell from 21% (2022) to 13% (2023) to 3.8% year-to-date 2026; code-block duplication is up 81% since 2023; and cross-file function connectivity has fallen 35% since 2023 (343 → 223 method calls per thousand changed lines), the structural signature of "duplicative reinvention" beating "progressively-improved reuse." 2024 was the first year on record that copy/paste exceeded moved code. SIG's *State of Software 2026* (30,000 enterprise systems): 86% of code falls below their recommended maintainability rating, 72% of AI systems in production score below recommended build-quality, and one case study saw autonomous agents build a system in a week while incurring €10–15M in token fees and producing "nearly unmaintainable" code. Roughly 42% of committed code is now AI-generated (Faros telemetry, cited across 2026 coverage) — the volume is real, so the discipline has to be systematic.

**Named practitioners converge on the same discipline.** Andrej Karpathy (who coined "vibe coding," Feb 2025) now urges keeping AI on a "tight leash" — many small generation-and-verification cycles, reading and accepting each diff before the next. At Sequoia Capital (Apr 30, 2026) he described the output candidly: "Sometimes I get a little bit of a heart attack because it's not like super amazing code necessarily all the time… It's very bloaty, and there's a lot of copy-paste, and there's awkward abstractions that are brittle, and it works, but it's just really gross." Armin Ronacher: present-day models produce code "too defensive, too complex, too local in its reasoning" — "They add fallbacks instead of making bad states impossible. They duplicate code, invent bad abstractions, and paper over unclear design with more machinery." Addy Osmani: the "70% problem" and "house of cards code" that "looks complete but collapses under real-world pressure." Simon Willison: "A computer can never be held accountable. That's your job as the human in the loop" — and he warns of a "normalization of deviance" as repeated AI success erodes review. Mitchell Hashimoto runs a mandatory "anti-slop" cleanup session and refuses to "ship code I don't understand." Kent Beck frames the AI as an "unpredictable genie" that grants wishes in perverse ways, kept in check by treating the test suite as an immutable contract.

**Open source is the canary — social backpressure applied.** curl ran its HackerOne/Internet Bug Bounty program from April 2019 to January 31, 2026, paying "over $90,000 for 81 discovered vulnerabilities" before Daniel Stenberg ended it ("The end of the curl bug-bounty," daniel.haxx.se, Jan 26 2026) after "death by a thousand slops." Stenberg's numbers: "Previous years we have had a rate of somewhere north of 15% of the submissions ending up confirmed vulnerabilities. Starting 2025, the confirmed-rate plummeted to below 5%. Not even one in twenty was real." Per Bugcrowd's account of his tracking, "in six years of monitoring submissions generated by AI alone, not a single one discovered a genuine vulnerability—zero." Removing the money was backpressure on intake incentives. QEMU, NetBSD, Zig, and Servo banned AI-generated contributions (largely on provenance/DCO grounds), though QEMU began relaxing toward disclosure-based limited acceptance in mid-2026 — a maintainer's key point: AI "reduces the cost of producing patches" but "does not reduce the cost of reviewing them and may even increase it."

## Details — the origins of the backpressure toolkit

The mechanisms below are not new; they are decades-old systems discipline. Michael Nygard's *Release It!* (2007) catalogued the stability patterns now standard: Timeouts, Circuit Breaker, Bulkheads, Fail Fast, Handshaking, Shed Load, and "Create Back Pressure" — each designed to stop a local failure from cascading. Kanban/Lean (Toyota; David Anderson's software adaptation) contributes WIP limits and Little's Law (John Little, 1961): *average cycle time = work-in-progress ÷ throughput*. Since your throughput (review capacity) is roughly fixed, every extra in-flight item lengthens everything. TCP flow control and reactive streams contribute the core idea of backpressure: a slow consumer signals a fast producer to slow down, rather than letting buffers grow unbounded. Every one of these maps directly onto agentic work — as a workflow discipline and as system architecture.

