## PATTERN CATALOG — PART A: SLOP-REDUCTION PATTERNS

### A1. Simplest-Thing-That-Works
**Attribution:** Karpathy ("tight leash"); Kent Beck ("tidy code that works").
- **Problem:** Agents have "an over-abundance of courage and show little to no taste for good code" (Karpathy); they default to the locally convenient answer and blow up complexity.
- **Rule:** Prompt for the minimal solution. "Write the simplest thing that works. No new abstractions, dependencies, or files without asking first." Constrain verbosity explicitly.
- **When to use / not:** Always for code you'll maintain. Relax only for genuine throwaway prototypes you will not ship.

### A2. Defensive-Coding Amplification Guard
**Attribution:** Armin Ronacher ("The Friction Is Your Judgment"; "Building Pi With Pi").
- **Problem:** "If you tell them that 'this malformed session log crashes the reader,' the clanker will often add a tolerant reader. Then it will add a fallback, then maybe a migration, then more debug output, then a test for all of this." Each looks reasonable in isolation; a hundred of them accrete into an unreasonable system.
- **Rule:** Tell the agent where the fix belongs. Prefer making bad states impossible (strong invariants) over adding fallbacks. Directive: "Do not add defensive fallbacks, tolerant readers, or error-masking without asking. Respect existing invariants."
- **When to use / not:** Any codebase with real invariants. Do *not* suppress genuine validation at true untrusted-input boundaries — the target is gratuitous local defense, not real boundary hardening.

### A3. The Anti-Slop Cleanup Session
**Attribution:** Mitchell Hashimoto ("Vibing a Non-Trivial Ghostty Feature," mitchellh.com, Oct 11 2025).
- **Problem:** Blindly accepting AI code produces comprehension debt; well-organized code also makes future agent sessions perform better.
- **Rule:** After the feature works, run a dedicated cleanup pass. Hashimoto: "To cleanup effectively you have to have a pretty good understanding of the code, so this forces me to not blindly accept AI-written code… I sometimes tongue-in-cheek refer to this as the 'anti-slop session'." Always end a session asking the agent what else it might be missing — with no code written in that step.
- **When to use / not:** Every non-trivial feature before shipping. Skippable only for disposable experiments.

### A4. Only-Ship-Code-You-Understand / Back-It-Out Rule
**Attribution:** Hashimoto; Simon Willison ("Your job is to deliver code you have proven to work").
- **Problem:** "Normalization of deviance" — repeated AI success erodes review discipline until you're merging code nobody reasoned through.
- **Rule:** If the agent fixes something and you don't understand the fix, back it out. Hashimoto: "If the agent figures it out and I don't understand it, I back it out. I'm not shipping code I don't understand… AI is no longer the solution; it is a liability."
- **When to use / not:** Always for anything touching other people's data or production. A deliberately-relaxed comprehension bar is acceptable only for personal throwaway tools.

### A5. Delete-Code Pass / Zero-Tolerance for Smells
**Attribution:** Kent Beck ("Tidy First?").
- **Problem:** Dead code, reinvented wheels, and inconsistent patterns accumulate silently; duplication imposes a "propagation tax" (GitClear) — change one copy, inherit the obligation to find and evaluate every sibling.
- **Rule:** Because fixing is now cheap, adopt zero tolerance. Run an explicit "what can be deleted?" pass each cycle. Flag duplication for consolidation, never copy-paste. Preserve optionality — don't let the agent "eat the seed corn" with poor design choices (Beck).
- **When to use / not:** Every review cycle. There is no "not" — cheapness of fixing removes the old excuse.

### A6. Sensors / Harness Engineering
**Attribution:** Birgitta Böckeler (Thoughtworks, "Harness Engineering," martinfowler.com, Apr 2026); Martin Fowler (caveat).
- **Problem:** Manual review doesn't scale to agent output volume; Böckeler names the "flow crisis" — humans becoming the congestion point — and "cognitive surrender," the point at which you stop caring and accept everything.
- **Rule:** Build computational "sensors" — linters, complexity metrics, duplication detectors, dead-code detection, architecture-fitness functions, and custom lint rules that catch your recurring slop patterns — that the agent runs against itself. Böckeler's model: feedforward (guides that prevent errors) + feedback (sensors that detect them) = a regulation system. Fowler's caveat is load-bearing: "Harness engineering is necessary. But it's the second line of defense, not the first. The first is specification engineering." A perfect harness verifying a vague spec just produces "garbage that's elegantly verified."
- **When to use / not:** Standing infrastructure for any repo an agent touches. Tools like SIG's Sigrid MCP check maintainability/security in-IDE as code is generated. Don't let sensors substitute for a clear spec.

### A7. Spec-Before-Code / Review the Plan
**Attribution:** Addy Osmani (spec-driven development, O'Reilly); Hashimoto.
- **Problem:** Vague requests produce plausible-but-hollow code. Osmani's thesis: "the AI is not the bottleneck. Your specification is." AI agents are "confidently wrong."
- **Rule:** Ask for a plan with no code first. Hashimoto: "It's important that I review a plan before it goes off and does a ton of work (and spends a ton of tokens doing it)." Spend the majority of effort on problem definition; inject "Known Pitfalls" into the spec (business rules, library gotchas, table relationships the training data can't know).
- **When to use / not:** Any vague or multi-step task. Overkill for a one-line, obviously-scoped fix.

### A8. Lean Agentic System Design (slop-reduction carried into what you build)
**Attribution:** 2026 multi-agent practitioner consensus; Google Research; Kent Beck (outcome-orientation).
- **Problem:** Over-engineered multi-agent architectures, tool sprawl, prompt bloat, premature framework adoption. "Most teams over-engineer toward multi-agent topologies before single-agent reaches its quality ceiling."
- **Rule:** Default to a single agent (ReAct baseline). Escalate to multi-agent only when single-agent caps out on a *measured* quality dimension. Google Research found 39–70% sequential-reasoning degradation from multi-agent coordination; teams that began with 3–5 agents and scaled on measured performance consistently outperformed teams launching with 10+. Coordination overhead can 5–10x cost and double latency without improving accuracy.
- **When to use / not:** Building production agentic systems. Add a second agent only when work decomposes cleanly, sub-tasks need different tools/models, or you need explicit safety/audit boundaries. Skip multi-agent entirely for linear pipelines and simple chatbots.

