## PATTERN CATALOG — PART B: BACKPRESSURE FOR THE WORKFLOW (the human-plus-agent loop)

### B1. WIP Limits on Agent Work
**Attribution:** Kanban; Hashimoto ("the mayor," ≤2 agents).
- **Problem:** Multiple agents in flight exceed human review capacity; by Little's Law every extra in-flight item lengthens the cycle time of everything.
- **Rule:** Cap concurrent agent tasks. Hashimoto manages "at most two agents at a time." Solo, 2–3 is the ceiling where flow survives (Personal Kanban converges on this; task-switching costs 20–40% of capacity). Iterative-Kanban guidance for agent loops: cap "Awaiting Human Review" at what one reviewer can clear (5–10 cards is a common team starting point; solo, far fewer). When the column is full, the only honest options are finish something or demote it to backlog.
- **When to use / not:** Always. Raise the limit only if review is genuinely idle. *(Hashimoto's specific "mayor"/two-agent framing traces to his Pragmatic Engineer podcast appearance via a secondary summary — directionally consistent with his written work, but treat the exact number as his practice, not a law.)*

### B2. Review-Capacity as the Bottleneck
**Attribution:** Little's Law; Goldratt's Theory of Constraints; LogRocket/MetaCTO framing.
- **Problem:** Throughput is limited by review, not generation. Optimizing generation while review is fixed produces the Faros paradox (98% more PRs, flat delivery).
- **Rule:** Treat your own review/comprehension bandwidth as *the* fixed constraint and size everything upstream to it. Move review earlier: approve specs/plans *before* generation, converting review from a discovery task ("is this correct?") into a verification task ("did it implement what we agreed?"), which is far faster.
- **When to use / not:** Always. This is the master frame the rest of Part B serves.

### B3. Circuit Breaker on Thrashing Agents
**Attribution:** Nygard; Osmani (the fix-creates-two-bugs spiral).
- **Problem:** Agents loop. Osmani: "The fix is gonna break something else. You're gonna ask AI to fix that issue and it's gonna create two more problems. Rinse, repeat. Sometimes it's five new problems."
- **Rule:** Halt the agent after N failed attempts on the same problem. Trip after ~3 strikes; then step back yourself, or back out any fix you can't explain (A4). Don't let it keep spending your review budget on a spiral.
- **When to use / not:** Any debugging loop. When it trips, the correct move is human diagnosis, not another prompt.

### B4. Timeouts / Small Diffs
**Attribution:** Karpathy ("the loop frequency matters more than the loop size").
- **Problem:** Long unattended runs produce worse code. Ronacher observes hands-off Claude Code working uninterrupted for 30+ minutes produces worse output than shorter human-in-the-loop cycles.
- **Rule:** Small incremental chunks; read and accept each diff before the next. Karpathy: "I always go in small incremental chunks… I want to spin this loop very, very fast."
- **When to use / not:** Production code. Longer autonomous runs are acceptable only for well-specified, measurable, low-stakes/throwaway tasks (Ronacher's "loops for exploration, interactive sessions for judgment").

### B5. Load Shedding Low-Value Tasks
**Attribution:** Nygard ("Shed Load"); Kanban backlog discipline.
- **Problem:** Agents generate candidate work faster than it can be verified; not all of it is worth doing.
- **Rule:** Explicitly shed/decline low-value tasks rather than queue them. Demoting a card back to backlog is the WIP limit working, not a failure. Shed when a task's expected value is less than the review cost it will impose.
- **When to use / not:** Whenever the review queue exceeds capacity.

### B6. Backpressure Signals — Stop and Ask
**Attribution:** Ronacher (scope-expansion critique); Hashimoto (hard interrupt).
- **Problem:** Agents "expand scope almost immediately," turning a narrow, fact-based bug into a large hypothesized surface area.
- **Rule:** Directive: "Surface before expanding scope. Do one thing at a time. Stop and ask after N failures." Keep the hard interrupt available — Hashimoto mid-session: "Stop stop stop. Undo all the main actor stuff." You choose when to engage; the agent doesn't get to drag you along.
- **When to use / not:** Always.

