## PATTERN CATALOG — PART C: BACKPRESSURE FOR AGENTIC SYSTEMS YOU BUILD

### C1. Bounded Queues Between Agents
**Attribution:** Nygard / reactive streams (TianPan, "Backpressure in Agent Pipelines," Apr 2026).
- **Problem:** Agent pipelines that spawn sub-agents and fan out tool calls create unbounded work queues. "An unbounded queue is just a memory leak with extra steps — it hides the problem by growing until something more catastrophic fails."
- **Rule:** Every buffer between planning and execution has fixed capacity; when full, the planner blocks. The planning LLM "does not know that the execution queue is 200 items deep or that you have hit 80% of your hourly token budget" — build that feedback loop explicitly.
- **When to use / not:** Any fan-out/orchestrator architecture. Unnecessary for a single linear agent.

### C2. Token/Cost Budgets as Flow Control
**Attribution:** Hierarchical budgets (TianPan; Claude Agent SDK; Runyard).
- **Problem:** Token costs compound non-linearly; a stuck overnight run turns "a hobby project into a horror story." Documented cases: an agent scanning a hobby network hit a $6,531 bill; a runaway aggregator agent burned $2,847 in 4 hours; late-loop steps can exceed 50K input tokens each.
- **Rule:** Hierarchical budgets — **job level** (hard ceiling, e.g. 500K tokens), **agent level** (a fraction of the job proportional to expected complexity), **function level** (strictest, e.g. 5K for a web search). When a level exhausts, execution stops *at that level* and does not cascade upward (the Linux cgroups principle). Set `max_turns` and `max_budget_usd` explicitly — the Claude Agent SDK ships both defaulting to *no limit*; its own docs say "Setting a budget is a good default for production agents," and the example sets `max_turns=30` "Prevent runaway sessions." The OpenAI Agents SDK raises `MaxTurnsExceeded`; treat `max_turns=None` as a loaded gun.
- **When to use / not:** Every autonomous agent, no exceptions. Quick win: a $10/day hard cap at the gateway catches most runaways before they matter.

### C3. Rate Limiting LLM Calls
**Attribution:** Token bucket / leaky bucket (TrueFoundry, BuildMVPFast, HiveMind arXiv).
- **Problem:** Per-user rate limits break at machine speed — one client's agent "sent 50,000 requests over six hours because no individual rate limit was ever breached. The per-user limit was 60 requests per minute. The agent never exceeded it. It just never stopped."
- **Rule:** Token bucket per *agent identity*; cost-based quotas; retry budgets; adaptive throttling (AIMD). Exponential backoff alone creates a thundering herd (all workers get the same 429, back off, and resume simultaneously, reproducing the overload). Parse provider rate-limit headers to pause proactively; keep a sliding-window RPM/TPM counter for providers that send no headers.
- **When to use / not:** Any production LLM pipeline. Roll out audit-only first (log what *would* be throttled), then enable on the highest-risk class — CI/CD agentic pipelines that have no human pacing mechanism.

### C4. Circuit Breakers Around Flaky Tools/Models
**Attribution:** Nygard (popularized by Netflix Hystrix; now Resilience4j/Polly/Opossum).
- **Problem:** "Without a circuit breaker, one bad minute cascades into minutes of queued failures." Cloud APIs drop packets, time out, rate-limit, and return 5xx.
- **Rule:** Track error rate over a rolling window; open the circuit at a threshold (e.g. 50% over the last 10 calls, or 5 consecutive failures); fail fast with a cached fallback or explicit "unavailable"; half-open probe after a timeout. A **token-rate** circuit breaker catches semantic loops that error-rate misses: a healthy agent rarely sustains >3,000–4,000 tokens/min because of I/O wait, so a 10,000 tokens/min threshold caught a runaway loop "within 60 seconds — well before any meaningful cost accumulated."
- **When to use / not:** Every external tool/model dependency. Not for in-process, local resources that can't hang independently.

### C5. Bulkheads Isolating Agent Failures
**Attribution:** Nygard (nautical watertight-compartment metaphor).
- **Problem:** When all agents/tools share one resource pool, one noisy workload exhausts threads/connections and breaks critical paths. Related real incidents: over-permissioned agents deleting thousands of legitimate records; credential sprawl across dozens of tokens.
- **Rule:** Separate resource pools per dependency — e.g. tightly cap expensive AI-model calls (5 concurrent), DB queries (20), email (10). One runaway agent cannot drain the whole system. Add a job-level budget kill switch and human-approval pause at the swarm level.
- **When to use / not:** Multi-agent/multi-tool production systems. Overkill for a single-tool agent.

### C6. Admission Control + Fallback Chains
**Attribution:** Nygard (admission control); TrueFoundry (declarative fallback).
- **Problem:** Under overload, hard-failing every request is the wrong outcome.
- **Rule:** Declarative per-route fallback chain (primary model → cheaper model → semantic cache → 503). Admission control temporarily rejects new work until capacity frees. Tune per route: a coding pipeline may prefer a clear failure over a degraded cheap-model result; a user-facing chatbot prefers any response.
- **When to use / not:** User-facing systems where degraded output beats no output. For correctness-critical pipelines, a clean fail can be safer than silent degradation.

