## DECISION RULES & THRESHOLDS
- **WIP limit:** 2–3 concurrent agent tasks solo; Hashimoto uses "at most two."
- **Circuit breaker (workflow):** trip after ~3 failed fix attempts; back out any fix you can't explain.
- **Circuit breaker (systems):** 50% error over last 10 calls, or 5 consecutive failures; sustained >10,000 tokens/min ≈ a loop (healthy agents idle at 3–4K/min).
- **Token budget:** job ≤500K tokens; per-agent a proportional fraction; $10/day gateway cap as the first guard; always set `max_turns` (e.g. 30) and `max_budget_usd`.
- **Duplication tripwire:** any new 5+ line duplicated block triggers review (GitClear's recommended "tripwire on duplicate blocks").
- **AI-code share:** ~42% of committed code is now AI-generated industry-wide; the practical band where review stays manageable for most teams is ~25–40% — above it, review becomes the binding constraint.
- **Diff size:** small enough to read every line honestly; split if not.
- **Shed a task:** when its expected value < the review cost it imposes.

## ANTI-PATTERNS & COUNTERS
- **Agent psychosis / in-group hype (Ronacher):** building decoupled from external validation — "for as long as nobody looks under the hood, you're good." *Counter:* outside review; ship to real users; sanity-check against reality.
- **Cognitive surrender (Böckeler):** accepting everything because volume overwhelms. *Counter:* WIP limits + sensors so you review *less but better*.
- **Automation bias (Zechner):** reviewing less after repeated success. *Counter:* fixed review discipline regardless of the agent's track record.
- **Slop loop / house of cards (Osmani):** the fix-creates-two-bugs spiral. *Counter:* circuit-breaker (3 strikes) + back-it-out rule.
- **Multi-agent over-engineering:** coordination overhead 5–10x cost, 39–70% reasoning degradation. *Counter:* single-agent default; escalate only on a measured ceiling.
- **Unbounded queue / no budget:** "the single most common failure mode in our audits: no budget caps." *Counter:* bounded queues + hierarchical budgets + gateway cap.
- **Slop PRs/reports to OSS (curl, QEMU, Servo):** generation is cheap, review is expensive — "a PR that took someone a minute to generate and takes an hour to review." *Counter:* disclose AI use, verify yourself with a real repro, respect project policy, and consider submitting the prompt, not just the code.

## STAGED ADOPTION PLAN
1. **Week 1 — Sensors on.** Add lint, duplication, dead-code, and complexity checks to the repo; wire SIG-style maintainability/security checks into the IDE/CI. Paste the anti-slop + backpressure blocks into AGENTS.md/CLAUDE.md. *Benchmark to watch:* duplication and complexity trend lines.
2. **Week 2 — Gate the intake.** Adopt plan-before-code, small diffs, and the Anti-Slop Review Gate. Institute the back-it-out rule. *Benchmark:* PR size stops growing; you understand 100% of merged lines.
3. **Week 3 — WIP + circuit breakers (workflow).** Cap concurrent agent tasks at 2; add the 3-strikes stop rule; make "what am I missing?" the mandatory closing step. *Benchmark:* review queue depth stays flat; no thrash spirals reach merge.
4. **Week 4 — Budgets + breakers (systems).** For any agentic system you build: set `max_turns`/`max_budget_usd`, bounded queues, per-tool circuit breakers and bulkheads; enable audit-only rate limiting, then enforce on the riskiest class. *Benchmark:* zero uncapped autonomous runs; no runaway cost events.
5. **Ongoing — Tune to metrics.** Track review-queue depth, duplication trend, churn (lines rewritten within 2 weeks), and incidents-per-PR. **Thresholds that change the plan:** if churn or incidents-per-PR rise while velocity climbs → tighten gates and lower WIP; if the review queue is chronically idle → you may raise WIP; if a class of mistake recurs → convert it into a lint rule/skill file rather than re-reviewing (A6).

## CAVEATS
- **Fast-moving tooling.** Model and harness behavior changes monthly. Ronacher notes mid-2026 hands-off harnesses sometimes produce *worse* code than late-2025 human-in-the-loop cycles. Re-tune every threshold here on a regular cadence; they are starting points, not constants.
- **Source reliability.** Vendor material (Faros, SIG, gateway vendors, orchestration startups) has commercial interest in the "review is the bottleneck / slop has cost" narrative. That direction is independently corroborated by an RCT (METR), longitudinal analysis (GitClear, 211M+ lines), and peer-style arXiv work — but treat specific vendor percentages as indicative rather than precise. Some practitioner details (notably Hashimoto's "mayor"/two-agent and "harness engineering" framing) reach this document through a secondary podcast summary; they are consistent with his primary blog writing but should be confirmed against the primary transcript before being quoted as exact.
- **Solo-context limits.** WIP numbers and Kanban/Little's Law framing come from team practice; adapted to one operator they are heuristics, not laws. The right WIP number is the one at which *your* review stays honest.
- **The core claim is durable even as numbers shift.** Generation outpaces both verification and comprehension. Slop-reduction constrains what the agent produces; backpressure paces and gates what it is allowed to do. Used together — on the code you write with agents and on the agentic systems you build — they are the controllable path. Used apart, you get either elegantly-verified garbage or an unbounded queue with a bill attached.
